Singapore’s tourism sector is experiencing a strong recovery with inbound tourists up 19 times compared to 2021.
(Singapore Tourism Board announces that international visitors to Singapore in 2023 will number between 12 million and 14 million, generating approximately S$18 billion to S$21 billion in tourism revenue; visitor numbers and tourism revenues will return to around 67-75% of pre-COVID levels in 2019; there is great likelihood for full recovery of the tourism sector in 2024.)
Singapore's tourism market will gradually restart and recover in 2022.
Singapore Tourism Board has recently released statistics showing that the number of inbound tourists increased 19 times from 330,600 in 2021 to 6.3 million in 2022, exceeding the earlier estimate of 4 to 6 million by the Tourism Board, and tourists have already recovered to 33% of the pre-pandemic level.
From January to September 2022, Singapore's tourism revenue totaled 8.96 billion Singapore dollars. It is expected that the tourism revenue in 2022 will reach 13.8 billion to 14.3 billion Singapore dollars, which is 6 to 7 times higher than 1.89 billion Singapore dollars in 2021, and has recovered to about 50% to 52% of the level before the COVID-19 pandemic in 2019.
The Singapore government has taken many steps to accelerate the recovery of tourism.
In April 2022, the government announced a grant of nearly NZ$500 million to stimulate the recovery of the tourism industry.
The Tourism Board recently announced that it plans to allocate NZ$110 million from this budget over the next two years to launch more commercial and leisure activities to attract tourists to return.
To make health and wellness one of Singapore's tourism highlights, the Singapore Tourism Board launched an expression of interest on November 23, 2022, to develop a health and wellness attraction on the southern coast of Singapore.
In recent years, more and more tourists have put their health first among all considerations, said Wang Linli, executive director of sports and wellness tourism at the Singapore Tourism Board.
The development of a health and wellness resort in the Hainan district of Singapore, which has the Hanging Bay Gardens and Hanging Bay Dams, is expected to contribute to Singapore's tourism and economy, allowing tourists to spend and stay longer in Singapore.
Singapore Tourism Board chairman, Xie Jianlong, said that health and wellness will be an important component of Singapore's tourism products in the next few years.
The coronavirus outbreak has raised public awareness and demand for physical and mental health.
To this end, the Tourism Board has been promoting health and wellness programs to the local public and international tourists over the past two years.
In June 2022, the Singapore Tourism Board held the first Health and Wellness Festival, offering the public more than 130 health activities and experiences.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences to visitors.
In addition to the newly opened Singapore Children's Museum, the Avatar World in Marina Bay Sands, the Central Beach Bazaar in Sentosa, the "Explore New Frontiers" exhibition added to the Art Science Museum, and the new open-air theater in the Night Safari, the Tourism Board will support the development and update of projects such as the Flying Penguins Park in the Wildlife World, making Singapore an attractive international travel destination.
As planned, Singapore will develop relevant industry standards for sustainable tourism conferences, exhibitions and awards by 2023, focusing on improving both waste management and carbon emissions, further optimizing the tourism environment.
The recovery of Singapore's tourism industry has accelerated the recovery of the aviation industry.
As of December 18, 2022, data shows that Changi Airport has a strong recovery in passenger flow, with more than 150,000 passengers received daily, about 1 million passengers received weekly, which is about 75% of the pre-pandemic level. The number of airline employees on duty has also recovered to about 90% of the pre-pandemic level.
Data shows that from January to October 2022, Singapore has received 23.6 million tourists, which is equivalent to 42% of the pre-pandemic period, and the take-off and landing flights were 170,000, which is 53% of the pre-pandemic period.
Tourism’s rapid rebound has also spurred recovery in sectors such as accommodation, transportation, dining, and shopping.
The Singapore government and tourism industry have high hopes for the Chinese market.
The assistant director of the International Department of the Singapore Tourism Board, Qiao Shudan, said that a survey conducted by a Chinese travel platform showed that Singapore ranked among the top three safest cities in the world, which is one of the reasons why Chinese tourists love Singapore.
Before the outbreak, about 3.6 million Chinese people visited Singapore for holidays every year.
As China relaxes border policies, it is expected that the number of Chinese tourists will recover to 30 to 60 percent of the 2019 level this year.
According to Qiao Shudan, in the future, the Tourism Bureau will closely monitor the signals of further relaxation of China's outbound travel policies to cope with the changing needs of tourists.
In addition to strengthening partnerships with Chinese online travel platforms and travel agencies such as Ctrip, the Singapore Tourism Board will actively prepare to receive Chinese tourists in response to new trends in outbound travel from China.
Looking ahead to the tourism market in 2023, the Singapore Tourism Board is confident that the global economy will recover faster than expected, as long as there is no significant recession affecting public income and no new large-scale fatal epidemic outbreaks.
A quick guide to internet finance
This book is very shallow; it can be considered an introductory course that will help you quickly understand the broad picture of internet finance but to really delve deeper, you need to follow the map and dig on your own.
And it was written earlier, while the later content of the WeChat and Alipay subsidy war to cultivate user habits has not been mentioned. Now, the proportion of the third-party payment is probably not so small as mentioned in the book.
Now many people go out to eat and just bring a mobile phone, and merchants have given up UnionPay because of the high bank flow.
But still, I am very grateful to the author for writing this introductory book; it is evident that the author has had many years of financial observation experience and is familiar with both domestic and international financial development histories, which allows him/her to write in such an easy-to-understand manner.
Writing highbrow is easy; making it accessible is hard.
